gripper stall_velocity_threshold noise-floor measurement — 2026-08-06
Purpose: controllers.yaml's gripper_controller.stall_velocity_threshold is
0.001 rad/s. Diagnosed earlier this session that the ROS action's own
stalled:true declaration takes 60-90s to arrive even though the physical
joint position settles within ~5s (docs/HANDOFF_M3.md, "06 retried post
spawn-state fixes" and the full-sweep follow-up). Hypothesis: 0.001 sits
below this sim's actual velocity readback noise floor at a contact-loaded
stall, so the required continuous 1.0s under-threshold window
(stall_timeout) rarely completes by chance. This log is the direct
measurement to check that, not a guess.

Setup: fresh sim (relaunched and health-checked earlier this session),
PRECLOSE=0.4523, 40mm box at the standard anchor spawn point
(0.49214 0.13332 1.11692), overclose commanded to 0.8 (backgrounded,
generous 100s bound so the action's own slow declaration doesn't interrupt
the measurement window). Waited 15s for physical settle (confirmed via gz
ground truth: 0.4589 rad at t=15s, consistent with the plateau already
established). Then sampled /joint_states directly via a dedicated rclpy
subscriber (scratchpad/sample_velocity_noise.py) for 30 wall-clock seconds
— not `ros2 topic echo` text parsing, to keep up with the 500Hz publish
rate (confirmed via `ros2 param get /controller_manager update_rate` = 500,
and `ros2 topic hz /joint_states` ~499Hz measured independently).

CAVEAT, stated not buried: the sampler captured 4374 of an expected ~15000
messages (29%) over the 30s window — under-sampled relative to the true
500Hz stream, likely rclpy executor/callback overhead in the polling loop,
not investigated further given session time. No evidence of directional
bias in which messages were dropped; treat the shape of the distribution as
indicative, not as a fully faithful 500Hz capture. Re-measuring with a
tighter/multi-threaded executor would be worth doing before finalizing a
threshold, not just before this log is trusted at face value.

RESULTS (robotiq_85_left_knuckle_joint, at a contact-loaded stall, 40mm box):

  samples: 4374
  position: min=0.456663 max=0.470407 rad, spread=0.013745 rad over 30s
            (small genuine physical drift/microvibration, not perfectly
            static even at "rest")
  velocity (rad/s): min=-0.127928 max=0.264404
  |velocity|: mean=0.014077 median=0.008250 stdev=0.037450 max=0.264404
    p50  0.008269
    p90  0.014186
    p95  0.025310
    p99  0.245261   <- sharp jump from p95, suggests occasional large
    p99.9 0.257012      single-tick spikes, not a smoothly-tailed distribution

  fraction of samples exceeding threshold:
    > 0.001 rad/s : 75.01%
    > 0.005 rad/s : 62.60%
    > 0.01  rad/s : 41.68%
    > 0.02  rad/s : 5.83%
    > 0.05  rad/s : 2.67%

READING: current stall_velocity_threshold (0.001) is exceeded by 75% of
individual samples even at a genuine, physically-settled contact stall.
This confirms the hypothesis directly, not just by inference from the slow
declaration observed earlier: the threshold cannot realistically be
satisfied for a full continuous stall_timeout (1.0s) window except by rare
chance, which is consistent with the 60-90s wait observed for the action's
own stalled:true result at this identical configuration.

The distribution has a clear "normal jitter" population (median 0.008,
p95 0.025) and a separate, much rarer "spike" population (p99 0.245, max
0.264) — roughly 1% of samples. A threshold picked to clear the p95 body
with margin (order 0.05 rad/s) would still occasionally be crossed by a
spike, but since stall_timeout only requires a CONTINUOUS 1.0s run below
threshold (not that every sample across all time stays below it), rare
single-tick spikes should just restart a short count rather than block
convergence for 60-90s the way the current threshold does — this is a
reasoning claim, not something this measurement directly confirms; the
actual convergence time under a candidate new threshold would need to be
checked against a real re-run once controllers.yaml is changed.

NOT DONE, deliberately, this session:
  - Did not change controllers.yaml. Setting stall_velocity_threshold is a
    real design decision (what margin, whether stall_timeout should also
    change, whether the "spike" population needs its own explanation before
    trusting a margin computed against it) that belongs to a session with
    room to validate the change live, not the tail of this one.
  - Did not re-measure with a faithful 500Hz capture to resolve the
    under-sampling caveat above.
  - Did not measure the noise floor during genuine ACTIVE motion (gripper
    still closing, before contact) for comparison — needed to confirm a
    candidate threshold sits safely BELOW real closing velocities too, not
    just above the at-rest noise floor. Without this comparison a threshold
    picked purely from the at-rest distribution could still be too low
    (false stalls during real motion) or, less likely given the margin
    involved, still not tight enough.
